# Oggie Spin — complete five-arm prototype

[`Oggie_Spin_Complete_P2S.3mf`](./Oggie_Spin_Complete_P2S.3mf) is the complete
printable spinner project for the Bambu Lab P2S.

[`Oggie_Spin_5x_Clip_Lock_P2S.3mf`](./Oggie_Spin_5x_Clip_Lock_P2S.3mf)
is the one-plate single-colour batch project. Its five meshes are separate
plate objects assigned to the same Matte filament and print **by layer**, with
zero filament changes. It uses 0.6 mm Slope Lift Z hop, 100% gyroid infill and
no prime tower.

[`Oggie_Spin_5x_Broken_Ring_P2S.3mf`](./Oggie_Spin_5x_Broken_Ring_P2S.3mf)
is the matching one-plate optical batch: five identical Marine Blue blocks with
the 25-dash broken-ring track embedded as flush Ivory White inlays. Map slot 1
to Marine Blue Matte and slot 2 to Ivory White Matte. It prints by layer with
0.6 mm Slope Lift Z hop, 100% gyroid infill, a prime tower and two colour
changes.

[`Oggie_Spin_Broken_Ring_Illusion_P2S.3mf`](./Oggie_Spin_Broken_Ring_Illusion_P2S.3mf)
is the optical winner on the matched clip-lock mechanism. Two 1.1 mm broken
tracks on the core (15 and 20 dashes) and one track on the five arms (25 dashes)
use flush 0.32 mm-deep Ivory White Matte inlays, plus an underside `BR`
identifier. Load the optical inlays in slot 7. Plates 1 and 5–9 use two colour
changes and a prime tower. Keep the 15/20/25 ring pattern unchanged; do not add
a fourth ring. Physical clip click, pinch-tab release, 100-cycle lock and
free-spin gates remain open.

Seven additional core-and-arm experiments live in
[`../optical-variants/`](../optical-variants/): full-body vortex (`VX`),
spiral/wave (`SP`), chevron (`CH`), strobe animation (`ST`), opposing drift
(`OD`), colour pulse (`CP`) and dashed ladder (`LD`). Each project has one core
plate, one batch-optimised five-arm plate and a flush two-letter identifier on
the core underside. They reuse this project's retaining ring, cartridge and
pads.

It contains nine prepared plates:

1. Ø40 core with five dovetail slots, underside-clip pockets and R188 seat
2. Press-fit outer-race retaining ring
3. Tough+ split-collet through-axle hub and through-bore receiver hub
4. Two identical 6.4 mm socketed thumb pads
5. Marine Blue wrap-around clip-lock segment
6. Lemon Yellow wrap-around clip-lock segment
7. Mandarin Orange wrap-around clip-lock segment
8. Grass Green wrap-around clip-lock segment
9. Lilac Purple wrap-around clip-lock segment

Each segment has identical geometry and mass. Outside the slot it wraps flush
around the core (R20.12–R24.5, ±20°) for the full 14 mm core height, so both
faces line up with the core. The three-rail tongue sits on the 2 mm shelf:
central dovetail (~0.16 mm/side) plus two tapered guide rails (~0.22 mm/side).
An integrated underside Matte PLA battery-cover cantilever clip snaps into a
core OD pocket for axial hold. Friction ribs, rigid side detents and legacy
barb recesses are retired. Core and arms are a matched revision — not
compatible with earlier solid-slide parts. Wrap clearance is 0.12 mm. The
tongue has a 0.4 mm bottom lead-in; cores also have a continuously lofted
0.4 mm slot-mouth lead-in. Reassign any arm plate to another Bambu Matte colour
without changing the mechanical design.

## Print configuration

- Bambu Lab P2S, 0.4 mm nozzle
- Bambu PLA Matte for the core, ring, pads and five colour blocks
- Bambu PLA Tough+ for both cartridge hubs
- 0.16 mm layers
- 5 walls on core / cartridge / pads / ring; 2 walls on clip-lock blocks
- 6 top and bottom layers
- 25% gyroid default infill
- 100% grid infill on all five colour blocks
- Supports off
- Clip-lock segments print flipped: flush top face on the bed so the slot
  tongue is not an unsupported overhang above the 2 mm shelf

All nine plates slice successfully in Bambu Studio 2.7.1 with empty warning
fields. The separate five-block plate also slices without warnings using:

- by-layer sequence for standard batch-print speed
- one shared Matte filament slot; zero colour changes
- 0.6 mm slope Z hop
- 0.8 mm / 30 mm/s retraction, layer-change retraction and 2 mm wipe
- avoid-crossing-wall travel with unlimited detour
- 2 walls, 6 top/bottom layers and 100% gyroid infill on every block
- no supports, brim or prime tower

## Additional hardware

- 1 × R188 bearing — 12.70 × 6.35 × 4.76 mm

The R188 is the only non-printed component.

## Assembly

1. Insert the R188 into the Ø12.82 core pocket from the top with controlled hand
   pressure until its outer race reaches the lower shoulder. This revision has
   0.12 mm nominal diametral clearance: Ø12.86 released under gravity, Ø12.68
   would not install by hand, and Ø12.78 remained too tight.
2. Press the Ø15.22 retaining ring down the wider counterbore until it contacts
   only the bearing outer race. The starting ring/counterbore interference is
   0.07 mm diametral.
3. Inspect the four Tough+ collet fingers. Reject the part if any root is
   cracked, fused across its 0.55 mm slots or visibly under-extruded.
4. Insert the split-collet hub from below. Its integrated Ø8.40 spacer stops on
   the R188 inner race. The revised Ø6.40 axle is a deliberate 0.05 mm nominal
   interference trial in the Ø6.35 bearing bore. It should enter with controlled
   hand pressure; stop rather than hammering or using pliers if it binds.
5. Place the receiver hub from above and press the two hubs together. The
   Ø6.80 collet bead compresses through the Ø6.48 receiver and must emerge
   fully beyond its outer face. Confirm approximately 0.05 mm axial freedom and
   a 1.6 mm hub-to-core gap on both sides.
6. Install either identical 6.4 mm pad on either side. Both carry the same
   Ø7.30 × 5.2 mm blind socket for batch-manufacturing uniformity; only the
   receiver side uses it to clear the exposed collet tip. Align each pad with
   its three hub gates, seat it fully by hand and rotate 30° into the terminal
   pockets. Pliers must not be required.
7. Align each three-rail tongue above a core slot and press vertically down by
   hand until the underside clip clicks into its core pocket.
8. Confirm every segment sits on its 2 mm bottom shelf, resists accidental lift
   in normal handling, and the outer wrap clears the core by about 0.12 mm.

To remove an arm, press the underside tip radially outward so the hook clears
the pocket, then lift the wrap vertically. To remove a thumb pad, rotate 30°
back past the detents and lift it off. To release the cartridge, remove the
upper pad, pinch all four exposed collet fingers inward, and push the axle back
through the receiver.

## Validation status

Generated checks confirm:

- 11 watertight printable meshes
- Nine-plate P2S 3MF with five Bambu Matte slots plus one Tough+ slot
- 100% infill overrides on exactly the five arm objects
- Only the intended underside-hook engagement on all five arms
- Zero unexpected installed arm interference after relieving the hook pockets
- Hook clears after 0.35 mm radial release travel
- Zero rigid-body interference in the installed printed cartridge
- 0.05 mm nominal axle/bearing diametral interference
- 0.08 mm axle/receiver diametral clearance
- 0.50 mm diametral and 0.50 mm axial clearance around the exposed collet tip
  inside the receiver-side pad
- Geometrically identical thumb-pad meshes
- Ø12.82 R188 pocket with 0.12 mm nominal diametral clearance
- Clear thumb-pad entry and terminal positions
- Controlled thumb-bayonet detent interference during rotation
- 49 mm assembled tip diameter (short wrap segments)
- Adjacent wrap segments clear each other at 72° pitch

Physical approval is still required. Before normal use:

- complete 100 arm insert/clip/release cycles;
- complete 100 thumb-pad lock cycles;
- complete 100 collet assemble/release cycles in Tough+;
- verify the retaining ring and R188 cannot move by hand;
- reject any collet with whitening, cracking, incomplete emergence or creep;
- confirm the printed cartridge does not increase bearing drag;
- reject any arm that shaves material, needs tools to install, fails to click,
  or releases accidentally in handling.

Do not use drill-driven or high-speed testing until these gates pass.

